BTRFS - B-tree FS, a copy on write filesystem.

Some basics:
    sudo pacman -S btrfs

    btrfs filesystem usage /home => subvolume name

    subvolumes share the same space and can consume the whole of the allocated disk.
    Quotas can be used to limit the usage of the disk for any given subvolume. !<Not completely stable>


    Snapshots
    btrfs subvolume snapshot tosnapshot pathtokeepit
    btrfs subvolume snapshot /home /home/home-snapshot
    btrfs subvolume list /home => get the id of the snapshot.

    Using the snapshot
    in the fstab file, instead of subvol=/home, use subvolid=261 and then reboot.
        261 is the assumed id of the snapshot home-snapshot.

    btrfs userspace utils.
    mkfs.btrfs device_identifier -L "label" -f

    btrfs subvolume create mountpoint subvol0, assuming mountpoint has sdc mounted.

    df -h = Not recommended for btrfs disks. Instead, use
    btrfs filesystem df mountpoint


